    narrative: "Yaman, also called Kalyan, is the most beloved of all evening raags and perhaps the most widely taught raag in Hindustani music. Its defining feature is the tivra (sharp) Madhyam, which gives the raag an open, luminous, reaching quality — as though the music is straining upward toward something just beyond grasp. Performed at the onset of night, Yaman embodies the ras of Shringar in its most refined, aesthetic sense: not the heat of longing but the beauty of the moment itself, the soft transition from day to dark. Its vadi Gandhar is approached with slow, deliberate meend, and the characteristic movement through Ni–Sa and Ma(t)–Pa gives it an effortless expansiveness that makes it a natural canvas for both long alap and ornate compositions. The ascent conventionally begins from the lower Ni — Ni Re Ga Ma(t) Dha Ni Sa — rather than from Sa, and Sa is skipped in this rise in every tradition. <em>Variance: Bhatkhande gives Ga as vadi and Ni as samvadi, and this is the standard reading; a minority of texts reverse them. Some traditions distinguish Yaman from Kalyan by the treatment of the lower Ni and the weight given to Pa, while most now treat the names as synonyms.</em>"
