    narrative: "Shankara is a pentatonic night raag invoking Lord Shiva, using the same five swaras as Hansadhwani but with an entirely different emotional world. Where Hansadhwani is bright and joyful, Shankara is austere and devotional, its phrases carrying weight and gravity appropriate to the deity it evokes. The absence of Madhyam and Dhaivata strips the raag down to its essence, and the characteristic movements — particularly the strong Ni–Sa and the deliberate treatment of Ga — distinguish it from its pentatonic cousin. It is a raag more heard in dhrupad than khayal."
