    narrative: "Miyan ki Malhar, attributed to the legendary Tansen of Akbar's court, is considered the supreme raag of the monsoon and one of the most emotionally profound raags in the entire tradition. Its use of both shuddha and komal Ni, sung consecutively in the phrase Ni(k) Dha Ni Sa, is its signature and captures the sound of rain itself; Ga is omitted in ascent and Dha in descent, and Ma is the vadi with Sa as samvadi. The raag has a quality of cosmic pathos — not personal sorrow but the vast, impersonal melancholy of the monsoon, the dissolution of summer's heat and dryness into the relief and sorrow of rain. Legend holds that Tansen sang this raag and caused rain to fall, and its power in performance remains undiminished across centuries. <em>Variance: The arohana is one of the most debated aspects — the Gwalior tradition uses a strict vakra ascending scale skipping Ga; the Agra gharana allows brief touches of Ga in ascent. The Ni–Ni(k) oscillation speed and extent varies: the Agra style uses a slow, wide meend between the two Ni variants while the Jaipur style is more contained. Some older sources give Pa or Ni as the vadi rather than Ma.</em>"
