    narrative: "Sur Malhar, or Surdasi Malhar, is attributed to the poet-saint Surdas and combines the Sarang ang with the Malhar ang. The ascent is Sarang — Sa Re Ma Pa Ni Sa — and the Malhar identity comes in the descent, where a prominent shuddha Dha separates it from the Sarangs: Ni(k) Dha Pa, Ma Re Pa, Ma Dha Pa. Komal Ga appears only lightly in descent, both Nishads are used, and the general movement circles the upper Sa. It is lighter and less profound than Miyan ki Malhar, and its compositions dwell on cloud, thunder and lightning. Where Miyan ki Malhar is the storm and Nat Malhar the exuberance, Sur Malhar is the rain falling in stillness."
