    narrative: "Desi is a late-morning raag of complex history: it was once called Deshi Todi, when the Todis still had a Bhairavi-like scale, and it keeps an Asavari-like ascent (Re Ma Pa Dha) beside its Kafi material. The most common form uses both Dhaivats; a second uses only shuddha Dha, and a third only komal Dha, which resembles Jaunpuri. Ga and Dha are avoided in direct ascent, the descent is oblique, the glide from upper Sa down to Pa is characteristic, and Pa and Re are the resting notes, with many phrases starting on Re. Parrikar treats it as a chalan-bheda of Kafi that 'requires significant training to master'. Bhimsen Joshi, Amir Khan and Kesarbai Kerkar all recorded it."
