    narrative: "Shahana Kanada is a late-night member of the Kanada group that, according to Faqirullah (1666), combined Amir Khusrau's now-obsolete Farudasht with Kanada. Pa and Sa are its sonant and consonant, and the emphasis is on the upper tetrachord, where shuddha Dha serves as a resting note always taken with Ni. The movements are oblique and full of glides; besides the Kanada patterns, phrases such as Ma Pa Ga(k) Ma recall Bhimpalasi and Bahar. It has a strong affinity with Bahar and Adana in the lower tetrachord, but the way the upper Sa is approached, and the rest on Dha rather than Ma, set it apart. Amir Khan, Basavraj Rajguru and Latafat Hussain Khan are among its recorded exponents."
