    sources: [{"tag":"RG","url":"https://archive.org/details/ragaguideasurveyof74hindustaniragassvarnalataraoetalled.joepbar"},{"tag":"T","url":"https://tanarang.com/raag-lalit/"},{"tag":"P","url":"https://www.parrikar.org/hindustani/lalitagouri/"},{"tag":"W","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lalit_(raga)"},{"tag":"CK","url":"https://chandrakantha.com/music-and-dance/i-class-music/index-of-rags/rag-lalit/"},{"tag":"M15","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4415143/"}],
    narrative: "Lalit is one of the most mysterious and searching raags in the entire canon, performed in the pre-dawn hours when the mind is at its most inward. Its use of both Madhyams — shuddha and tivra side by side — along with the complete absence of Pa creates a raag of profound harmonic strangeness: without Pancham, the usual sense of tonal anchor is removed, and the music floats in a way that is both unsettling and beautiful. Shuddha Ma is the vadi. The komal Re and komal Dha add further weight; with komal Dha, as it is sung today, Lalit sits in the Purvi thaat, and with the shuddha Dha that Bhatkhande recorded as canonical it belongs to Marwa. Lalit is considered one of the most difficult raags to perform convincingly and one of the most rewarding to hear."
