    narrative: "Puriya Kalyan sets Puriya in the lower tetrachord and Kalyan (Yaman) in the upper: komal Re and tivra Ma below, a full shuddha Dha, Ni and a sustained Pa above. Sa is avoided in ascent, so phrases begin on the lower Ni, and Pa is avoided in ascent but often used as a resting note. Ga and Ni are strong, and Ni Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Dha Ga is a characteristic movement. The Raga Guide traces the name to Hridayanarayana at the end of the 17th century; Bhatkhande called the raag Purva Kalyan, and the modern form was partly reinvented in the 20th century. It is sung in the early evening, between Marwa's sunset hour and Yaman's night."
