    narrative: "Chandrakauns is one of the most hypnotic and ethereal raags in the tradition, a late-night pentatonic raag that omits Re and Pa and uses Sa, komal Ga, Ma, komal Dha and shuddha Ni. It is Malkauns with the Nishad raised: that single change is the entire difference between the two, and the shuddha Ni — leaning up toward Sa instead of hanging below it — is what gives Chandrakauns its silvery, unresolved brightness where Malkauns is dark and settled. The absence of Pa leaves the raag floating without a stable fifth, and Ma is the vadi. Named after the moon (Chandra), it is a favourite of sitar and sarod players for its emotive power. By scale it fits none of the ten thaats cleanly; it is filed under Kafi or Bhairavi by convention."
